---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import insights from "../../data/insights.json";

const insight = insights.find((item) => item.slug === "what-we-learned-building-api-governance-with-open-standards");
const publishedDate = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-GB", {
  day: "numeric",
  month: "long",
  year: "numeric",
}).format(new Date(`${insight?.date}T12:00:00Z`));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What We Learned Building API Governance with Open Standards"
  description="A practitioner story from APIOps Helsinki with Aleksei Popov on starting API governance with shared problems, open standards, and automation."
  type="article"
  publishedTime={insight?.date}
  author={insight?.author.name}
  image={insight?.ogImage}
  imageAlt={insight?.image.alt}
  imageWidth="1200"
  imageHeight="630"
>
  <nav class="community-nav">
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a class="brand" href="/">
        <img class="brand-logo" src="/favicon.svg" alt="APIOps logo" />
        <strong>APIOps Insights</strong>
      </a>
      <div class="nav-links">
        <a href="/insights">Insights</a>
        <a href="/events">Events</a>
        <a href="/">Community</a>
        <a class="button primary" href="#subscribe-section">Join the Community</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <main class="community-page">
    <article class="insight-article">
      <header class="insight-article-hero">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="insight-article-meta">
            <a href="/insights">Insights</a>
            <span>{insight?.category}</span>
            {insight?.communities.map((community) => <a href={community.href}>{community.name}</a>)}
            <time datetime={insight?.date}>{publishedDate}</time>
            <span>{insight?.readTime}</span>
          </div>
          <h1>What We Learned Building API Governance with Open Standards</h1>
          <p class="insight-subtitle">
            {insight?.subtitle}
          </p>
          <p class="insight-byline insight-byline-on-dark">
            <span>Author</span>
            <a href={`/authors/${insight?.author.slug}`}>{insight?.author.name}</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="container insight-article-shell">
        <aside class="insight-aside">
          <span>Insights</span>
          <a href="/insights">Practitioner Stories</a>
          <a href="/insights">Patterns & Lessons</a>
          <a href="/insights">APIOps Cycles in Practice</a>
          <a href="/insights">Event Notes / Synthesis</a>
        </aside>

        <div class="insight-body">
          <figure class="insight-media insight-media-lead">
            <img
              src={insight?.image.src}
              alt={insight?.image.alt}
              width="1600"
              height="1600"
              fetchpriority="high"
            />
            <figcaption>API governance and APIOps Cycles in practice at APIOps Helsinki.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>API Governance Doesn't Have to Start With a Platform</h2>
          <p>
            When organisations decide they need better API governance, it is surprisingly easy to end up shopping
            for technology before agreeing on what problem governance is supposed to solve.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the September <a href="/communities/finland">APIOps Helsinki</a> meetup, Aleksei Popov shared a very
            practical story of building API governance from a considerably less-than-perfect starting point: more than
            2,000 endpoints, three API versions, four authentication methods, several names for the same parameters,
            and no common API standards or clearly defined ownership.
          </p>
          <p>The initial challenge wasn't a lack of available solutions. Quite the opposite.</p>
          <p>
            Once you start researching API governance, you quickly encounter API strategy, management, gateways,
            developer portals, security, productization and a long list of commercial platforms. Evaluating all those
            alternatives can easily become another problem of its own. Aleksei described this rather accurately as
            <strong>"analysis paralysis."</strong>
          </p>

          <h3>Start with the problems, not the tools</h3>
          <p>
            Instead of beginning with a large technology selection exercise, the team started breaking the problem into
            manageable pieces.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/#cycles">APIOps Cycles</a> provided a way of looking at APIs from the perspective of the
            organisation and the API lifecycle, including questions such as who the API is for, what business
            opportunities it supports and what product owners need to understand about APIs.
          </p>
          <p>From there, the work could become much more concrete.</p>
          <p>For each area, the team could ask:</p>
          <blockquote>
            Where are we now? What does good look like? What happens if we don't change? What do we gain if we do? How
            could we get there, and how important is it?
          </blockquote>
          <p>That is a much easier conversation to have than "Which API management platform should we buy?"</p>

          <figure class="insight-media insight-media-map">
            <a href="/#cycles">
              <img
                src="/images/metro_map.svg"
                alt="APIOps Cycles metro map showing the connected areas of API operating-model work"
                width="1000"
                height="1000"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption>
              The APIOps Cycles metro map provides a shared view of API work across business, governance, platforms,
              delivery, and adoption. <a href="/#cycles">Explore APIOps Cycles</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>Governance became something teams could actually use</h3>
          <p>The result wasn't a giant governance programme.</p>
          <p>
            The API team created a company API Style Guide, introduced CI automation for API contracts and testing,
            applied the governance approach while building APIs themselves, migrated an existing product API to the new
            process, created documentation and AI-assisted guidance, and helped other teams build and support their
            APIs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Open standards and open-source tooling played an important role. The implementation used OpenAPI together
            with tools such as Spectral for linting, oasdiff for detecting breaking changes and Schemathesis for
            testing, supported by CI/CD automation. The stack also generated interactive documentation, a Python SDK
            and a CLI.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is an important distinction:
            <strong>
              governance wasn't just documentation telling teams what they should do. It increasingly became part of the
              way APIs were built.
            </strong>
          </p>

          <figure class="insight-media insight-media-community">
            <img
              src="/images/insights/api-governance-open-standards/apiops-helsinki-community.jpg"
              alt="Practitioners listening to a talk at an APIOps Helsinki meetup"
              width="1600"
              height="1600"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>Practitioners gathering to compare real API governance experiences at APIOps Helsinki.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>You can start surprisingly small</h3>
          <p>
            Perhaps the most useful lesson from Aleksei's talk was that improving API governance doesn't require
            solving everything first.
          </p>
          <p>
            His suggested starting point is refreshingly concrete: draft an API Style Guide, start linting and testing
            OpenAPI definitions, and then work on adoption through documentation, shared automation, AI skills and
            internal workshops.
          </p>
          <p>
            For organisations currently debating governance models, tooling or API management platforms, that's worth
            remembering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>
              You don't need to have your final API governance architecture figured out before you start improving API
              governance.
            </strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Start with the problems you actually have. Establish shared practices. Automate what can be automated. Try
            them on real APIs. Learn what works.
          </p>
          <p>Then iterate.</p>
          <p class="insight-credit">
            Aleksei Popov presented "What I Learned Building API Governance with Open Standards" at
            <a href="/communities/finland">APIOps Helsinki</a> 2026.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
